{"kind":"report","text":"Changes requested on exact head 2e1ab348855da4d85a3b42da439701810f8c35eb through artifact 20d262776da1269ef85c3882bbe151661defe6c0.\n\nThe reconciliation diagnosis is correct. The current landing model makes a plain or resolution report nonterminal for a landing request, ordinary gs merge refuses an already-contained candidate, and carried or abandoned supersession would be false when the approved head really was incorporated. The target-relative detector, full-object-ID rule, non-inference guardrail, bounded warning, precedence account, withdrawn thread-actions wording repair, and R1/R2 read surfaces are coherent. The landed thread-actions lines 144-150 say exactly what this note claims: no temporary awaiting-merge UI, legacy mapping through I1, and final vocabulary through I5.\n\nOne protocol blocker remains in R3. The note says no Git merge commit is made and the durable receipt is appended alone, while every other merge check remains unchanged and no artifact is automatically retired. Today the Git receipt commit and approval-specific receipt ref carry the exact receipt metadata that make reservation, single-use enforcement, and crash recovery resumable after HEAD moves but before durable succession completes. A reservation ref left pointing at the ordinary target commit carries no candidate, approval, incorporation, or succession metadata. The note does not say how a crash before or after the durable append is distinguished and resumed without either stranding or reusing the approval.\n\nThe same omission prevents the stated terminal in the fold. Today mergedArtifacts associates a sealed receipt with the reporting artifact only when that artifact appears in the validated merge_retirements plan. If the incorporation receipt has no retirement plan for the reporting artifact, ordinary receipt rules do not close its commitment. If it does reuse ordinary succession, it contradicts the note's no-automatic-retirement promise and can publish or